 because what we have here is a copy of  the statue of the fawn from the interior  of the house of the fog  as you entered the house of the fawn the  the first main room is the atrium this  is standard in these roman style houses  and in the atrium there's this little  statuette of the fawn and it faces the  doorway we've got it placed so you can  see it as you would entering the house  in antiquity  and it's a marvelous opportunity because  you get this sense of what this is like  this fawn really represents the  integration of greek culture into roman  domestic space even at the very highest  levels because this was the house of the  patron of the colony  sulla the nephew of the dictator and  this is the image that greets you when  you walk in is an image of dionysiac  greek mythology are fawn so the fawn is  something of a touchstone here  the walls lining the streets of pompeii  were typically plastered and whitewashed  to discourage burglars most houses  lacked large ground level windows  resulting in long tempting stretches of  white surfaces  these expanses were exploited by a wide  spectrum of people who painted or  incised messages ranging from the  political to the personal  much graffiti was related to commercial  activity it appears to have been common  practice to advertise rooms for rent by  painting a sign on the wall  one example for upscale property states  for rent the arius polio apartment  building owned by nias alias [ __ ]  mayas  street front shops with counter space  luxury second-story apartments and a  townhouse available beginning july 1st  interested parties should contact primus  the slave of nias alias nigidis mayas  pompeii's electoral graffiti even supply  a glimpse of the  dirty tactics that some politicians  might employ  one strategy was to slander your  opponent by putting up graffiti  that appeared to offer endorsements of  him by undesirable groups  the enemies of a man named vatia seem to  have been particularly fond of this  tactic they painted a number of such  notices including  the petty thieves ask you to elect  vadiya's aedile  and the late sleepers ask you to elect  vadiya's adile  and also all the drunkards ask you to  elect marcus cyrinius vatia-edile papaya  was born in about 31 and grew up in the  pompeii region in southern italy  pompeii was a beautiful prosperous town  overlooking the sparkling bay of naples  with the smoking mount vesuvius in the  background  the volcano wouldn't bury the town until  48 years later when papaya grew up there  it was a perfect roman town with about  11 000 people  it was prosperous and dotted with  beautiful villas as visitors to the  ruins today can see  as soon as papaya became pregnant with  nero's child the emperor divorced  octavia when the people of rome  protested he had octavia banished to an  island and then murdered  nero had octavia's head delivered to  papaya  i suppose she saw it as an engagement  gift  the people of rome rioted at the death  of octavia she was popular and  considered a worthy family member of the  divine augustus in their riots they  destroyed the statues of papaya blaming  her for the death of octavia  reputedly papaya didn't care  she was consolidating her power over the  young ruler  how has posterity remembered papaya  the ancient historian josephus portrayed  her as a pious religious seeker  while tacitus and others remember her as  ambitious  ruthless and murderous  modern portrayals in art and movies from  quavadas to i claudius and many others  emphasize her beauty sexuality and  ruthlessness  as well as her abuse by the notorious  emperor  at the end of the day papaya expresses  the life of many men and women at the  heights of power of the roman empire  the best of them were lured in device  and murder by the huge power of the  imperial title  the worst of them indulged their vices  but still  sometimes expressed their humanity  few however had the long-standing  influence of papaya whose combination of